<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Quetzaltenango</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Xela, Xelajú</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Quetzaltenango, called Xelajú or Xela, is the second-largest city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guatemala">Guatemala</a>, in the western highlands. Near it <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pedro-de-alvarado">Pedro de Alvarado</a> defeated the K'iche' in 1524, and it became a Spanish town. From 1838 to 1840 it was the capital of the breakaway State of Los Altos until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rafael-carrera">Rafael Carrera</a> reconquered it, and it later prospered from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coffee-economy">coffee</a> economy.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/quetzaltenango.jpg" alt="Quetzaltenango" width="320" height="205" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: PabloRosado · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Palacio_Municipal_de_Quetzaltenango.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Quetzaltenango is the capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">department</a> of the same name in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guatemala">Guatemala</a>. It stands at an elevation of 2,330 metres in a high-altitude volcanic basin of the Sierra Madre, encircled by the volcanic complexes of Santa María, Santiaguito and Cerro Quemado. It is part of the Los Altos Metropolitan Area, which includes Salcajá, Cantel, Almolonga, Zunil and other municipalities.</p><p>The name is <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nahuatl">Nahuatl</a> and means the walled place of the quetzal bird; it became the city's official name in colonial times. Many of its people, especially the indigenous population, use the Kʼicheʼ name Xelajú, or Xela, from xe laju' noj, meaning under ten mountains. Some consider it, unofficially, the capital of the Mayas.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Before the Spanish conquest Quetzaltenango was a city of the Mam Maya, though by 1524 it had become part of the Kʼicheʼ Kingdom of Qʼumarkaj. It was said to have been over 300 years old when the Spanish arrived. With the help of his allies, the conquistador <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pedro-de-alvarado">Pedro de Alvarado</a> defeated and killed the Maya ruler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tecun-uman">Tecún Umán</a> there.</p><p>From 1838 to 1840 the city was the capital of the state of Los Altos, one of the states of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/federal-republic-of-central-america">Federal Republic of Central America</a>. As the union broke up, the army of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rafael-carrera">Rafael Carrera</a> conquered the city and made it part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guatemala">Guatemala</a>. In 1850 it had about 20,000 people, and wheat was its largest export, followed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cacao">cacao</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a>, wool and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a>. During the 19th century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coffee-economy">coffee</a> was introduced as a major crop, the local economy prospered, and much Belle Époque architecture survives in the city.</p><p>On 24 October 1902 the Santa María volcano erupted, and rocks and ash fell on the city an hour later. In 1930 the only electric railway in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guatemala">Guatemala</a>, the Ferrocarril de Los Altos, was inaugurated, built by AEG and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krupp">Krupp</a> and linking Quetzaltenango with San Felipe, Retalhuleu. Mudslides soon destroyed it and it was demolished in 1933. A railway museum now stands in the city centre.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Quetzaltenango is the main economic, educational and cultural centre of the south-western Guatemalan highlands. At the 2018 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> it had 180,706 inhabitants, and 43 percent of its population was indigenous Guatemalan in 2014. It is home to several universities, among them the Centro Universitario de Occidente San Carlos de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guatemala">Guatemala</a>, and to the football club Club Xelajú MC. In 2008 the Central American parliament, PARLACEN, announced that the city would be the capital of culture of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-america">Central America</a> every 15 September.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Quetzaltenango” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Rafael Carrera’s rising, supported by the clergy and conservatives, broke liberal control of the state, and in 1838 western Guatemala broke away as the State of Los Altos, with <b>Quetzaltenango</b> as its capital.</blockquote><a href="/empire/federal-republic-of-central-america">Federal Republic of Central America · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1524 Pedro de Alvarado defeated the K'iche' near <b>Quetzaltenango</b>, where tradition says the hero Tecun Uman fell, then burnt the kings Oxib Keh and Beleheb Tz'i and destroyed Q'umarkaj.</blockquote><a href="/state/kiche-kingdom">K'iche' kingdom</a></li><li><blockquote>Ladino and criollo elites of <b>Quetzaltenango</b> and the western highlands had long resented domination by Guatemala City.</blockquote><a href="/state/state-of-los-altos">State of Los Altos</a></li></ul></section>
